---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/paths';

const PDF = '/downloads/camping-checklist-printable.pdf';
const CHECKOUT_URL = 'https://whop.com/best-out-door-wears/new-camp/';

const sections = [
  {
    name: 'Shelter & sleep',
    note: 'The bit that decides whether you sleep or endure.',
    items: [
      ['Tent, poles, pegs', 'Count the pegs at home, not in the dark'],
      ['Groundsheet or footprint', ''],
      ['Sleeping bag', 'Rated for the coldest night, not the average'],
      ['Sleeping mat', 'Insulation from the ground, not just comfort'],
      ['Pillow', 'A stuff sack of clothes works'],
      ['Mallet', ''],
      ['Spare pegs and 3m of cord', 'Weighs nothing, saves a night'],
      ['Tarp for the cooking area', 'Turns a wet weekend into a normal one'],
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Kitchen & food',
    note: 'Plan the washing-up before you plan the menu.',
    items: [
      ['Stove and fuel', 'Check the fuel fits the stove'],
      ['Lighter, plus matches as backup', ''],
      ['Pan, plate, bowl, mug per person', ''],
      ['Knife, spoon, spatula', ''],
      ['Chopping board', ''],
      ['Washing-up bowl, sponge, soap', ''],
      ['Water container with a tap', 'Check if the site has drinking water'],
      ['Food, by meal not by guess', 'Write the meals down, then shop'],
      ['One no-cook meal', 'For the night you arrive late or wet'],
      ['Rubbish bags', ''],
      ['Coolbox and ice packs', 'Chill the box before you load it'],
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Clothing',
    note: 'You sit still for hours after dark. Pack for that, not for the walk.',
    items: [
      ['Base layer — wool or synthetic', 'Not cotton next to skin'],
      ['Fleece or mid layer', ''],
      ['Waterproof jacket', 'Wash and re-proof it if it stopped beading'],
      ['Waterproof trousers', 'Also a windproof leg layer'],
      ['Warm layer for the evening', ''],
      ['Hat and gloves', 'Even in summer, at night'],
      ['Spare socks — two pairs', 'The most forgotten item on any list'],
      ['Camp shoes', 'Something other than the boots you walked in'],
      ['Sun hat and sunscreen', ''],
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Light, tools & safety',
    note: 'One shared torch means three people sitting in the dark.',
    items: [
      ['Head torch per person', ''],
      ['Spare batteries', 'Keep them somewhere warm'],
      ['Lantern for the group', ''],
      ['First aid kit', 'Blister and burn cover matter most at camp'],
      ['Any personal medication', ''],
      ['Power bank, charged', ''],
      ['Offline map, plus a screenshot', 'A screenshot survives a dead signal'],
      ['Whistle', 'Carries further than your voice'],
      ['Multi-tool or knife', ''],
      ['Duct tape wrapped round a bottle', ''],
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Personal',
    note: '',
    items: [
      ['Toothbrush and toothpaste', ''],
      ['Towel', ''],
      ['Toilet roll', 'Never assume the site has any'],
      ['Hand sanitiser', ''],
      ['Insect repellent', ''],
      ['Sealed bag for wet or dirty kit', ''],
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Before you drive off',
    note: 'The six that cost nothing and prevent the most.',
    items: [
      ['Someone knows where you are going', 'And when you are due back'],
      ['Booking confirmation saved offline', ''],
      ['Tent pitched once at home', 'Do not learn it in the dark, in wind'],
      ['Fuel, tyres, spare tyre', ''],
      ['Weather checked for the nights', 'Not just the days'],
      ['Local fire rules checked', 'Open fires are banned in many places'],
    ],
  },
];

const total = sections.reduce((n, s) => n + s.items.length, 0);

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: [
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'I\'m going camping for the first time — what do I actually need to bring?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'For a first tent or car camping trip you need six things covered: shelter and sleep (tent, pegs, sleeping bag, insulating mat), kitchen and food (stove, fuel, one pan, water container, meals planned by name), clothing in layers with two spare pairs of socks, a head torch for every person, a first aid kit, and someone at home who knows where you are going and when you are back. Everything else is comfort. The full 50-item list on this page is free to read and free to download, with no email required.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'I\'m going camping this weekend. What should I pack?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Work through a checklist a few days before rather than the night before, because that is when you still have time to fix an empty gas canister or a flat head torch battery. Pack shelter and sleep first, then food planned meal by meal, then clothing in layers including a warm layer for sitting still after dark, then light and safety. Cross off anything that does not apply to this specific trip — a checklist is a memory aid, not an instruction to bring all fifty things.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'What do people always forget when camping?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'The most commonly forgotten items are spare dry socks, enough light for everyone rather than one shared torch, a warm layer for sitting still after sunset, rubbish bags, a spare tent peg and a length of cord, and toilet roll. None of them are expensive and each one is remembered vividly by whoever forgot it.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Is there a free printable camping checklist I can just download?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Yes — this page has one. It is a two-page PDF with 50 items across six sections, free with no email address, no signup and no account. It also works as a real form: the checkboxes are genuine PDF form fields, so you can tick them on screen and save the file, or print it and use a pen. Most free printable checklists are flat images whose boxes cannot be ticked at all.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'What\'s the difference between a camping checklist and a backpacking checklist?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'A camping checklist assumes a car carries the weight, so it can include a cool box, a chair, a bigger tent and a proper cooking set. A backpacking checklist assumes everything goes on your back, which changes every trade-off: you drop comfort items, carry a filter instead of water, and think in grams. Using a car camping list for a backpacking trip is how people end up with a pack too heavy to enjoy.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'How do I make a camping checklist for my family?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Start from a general list, then add one line per person for the things that are individual — medication, glasses, a comfort toy for a small child, a head torch each. Keep shared items in one place on the list so nobody packs three stoves and no pan. After the trip, edit the list while it is fresh: cross off what you never touched and add what you wished you had. After two or three trips it fits how your family actually camps.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'What should I wear camping at night when it gets cold?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Pack for sitting still, not for walking. Camping is the one outdoor activity where you spend hours doing almost nothing after dark, which is exactly when the temperature drops fastest — so bring a warmer layer than you think, a hat, and something on your feet other than the boots you walked in. Dry socks matter more than an extra jacket, because feet that got damp during the day will feel cold all evening.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Do I really need a sleeping mat, or is the sleeping bag enough?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'You need the mat. Cold ground draws heat out of you directly, and your body weight flattens the sleeping bag\'s insulation underneath you to almost nothing, so the bag cannot do its job where you are lying on it. A modest bag on a good insulating mat is warmer than an expensive bag on a thin one.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'How far in advance should I pack for a camping trip?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Run the checklist a few days before, then again as you load the car. The early pass is what catches the problems you can still solve — empty stove fuel, dead torch batteries, a tent you have never pitched. The second pass is what stops things being left on the kitchen table.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'What is the one thing I should not skip before a camping trip?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Tell one person where you are going and when you expect to be back, and send it as a message so it is written down rather than remembered. It costs nothing, weighs nothing, and it is the single thing that most shortens a search if anything goes wrong. Pitching your tent once at home before you go is a close second.' },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Camping Checklist: Printable, Free, and the Boxes Actually Tick"
  description="A free printable camping checklist — 50 items across six sections, real PDF checkboxes to tick on screen or print. No email required."
  canonicalPath="/camping-checklist/"
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <section class="checklist-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="label">Free download · No email required</span>
      <h1>The camping checklist,<br>and its boxes actually tick</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Fifty items across six sections, built for tent and car camping. Print it and use a pen, or
        tick the boxes on screen and save the file — because most free printable checklists are flat
        images whose boxes do nothing at all.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={url(PDF)} download>Download the PDF — free</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#list">Read it on this page ↓</a>
      </div>
      <p class="affiliate-note" style="margin-top:16px; max-width:60ch;">
        Two pages, 50 fillable checkboxes. Nothing to sign up for, and you're welcome to pass it on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="list">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>All {total} items,<br>free to read here</h2>
        <p class="desc">The same list that's in the PDF. Nothing held back for the download.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cl-grid">
        {sections.map((s) => (
          <div class="cl-section">
            <h3>{s.name}</h3>
            {s.note && <p class="cl-note">{s.note}</p>}
            <ul class="cl-list">
              {s.items.map(([item, why]) => (
                <li>
                  <span class="cl-item">{item}</span>
                  {why && <span class="cl-why">{why}</span>}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="product-section">
    <div class="wrap prose" style="max-width:74ch;">
      <h2>Why most printable checklists disappoint</h2>
      <p>
        Search for a printable camping checklist and you get a lot of results, most of them from
        companies that sell camping gear. That shapes them in two ways worth knowing about.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, the lists are long because a long list is a long shopping list. Every line is
        something you could buy, and nothing on them ever says "you probably already own this" or
        "skip it". Second, the files themselves are usually flat print images. The boxes are drawn
        on, not real — so you cannot tick them, and tracking your packing means a printer and a pen.
        Try it on one and see.
      </p>
      <p>
        This one is short on purpose, tells you why an item matters where that isn't obvious, and its
        boxes are genuine PDF form fields. We don't sell gear, so nothing here is trying to get you
        to buy a second stove.
      </p>

      <h2>How to use it without overpacking</h2>
      <p>
        Work through it once a few days before the trip, not the night before — that's when you find
        out the stove fuel is empty and the head torch batteries are flat, while there's still time to
        do something about it. Then run it again as you load the car.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cross off anything that doesn't apply to this specific trip. A checklist is a memory aid, not
        an instruction to bring all fifty things. The six items under "before you drive off" are the
        ones worth never skipping, and five of them cost nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>What this list doesn't cover</h2>
      <p>
        It's built for tent and car camping, where you can carry a coolbox and a chair because the
        car does the carrying. Backpacking needs a different list, because every item goes on your
        back and the trade-offs change completely — that's covered in the
        <a href={url('/articles/backpacking-packing-checklist/')}>backpacking checklist</a>, along with
        how to actually load the pack.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also doesn't tell you what to wear, beyond the basics above. Clothing depends on the
        forecast and the season, which is a longer subject —
        <a href={url('/articles/how-to-layer-clothes-for-hiking/')}>the layering guide</a> covers it,
        and <a href={url('/articles/what-to-wear-camping-in-fall/')}>what to wear camping in fall</a>
        deals with the season that catches people out most.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>The paid version,<br>if you want the whole trip</h2>
        <p class="desc">This page covers a normal camping trip. TrailReady covers five of them, plus what to do when it goes wrong.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-includes" style="max-width:74ch;">
        <div class="item"><span class="n">01</span><div><h3 class="item-h">Five trip types, five lists</h3><p>Day hike, overnight backpacking, car camping, cold weather and wet weather — each with its own chapter and its own fillable list.</p></div></div>
        <div class="item"><span class="n">02</span><div><h3 class="item-h">The Conditions Helper</h3><p>A working spreadsheet. Pick the trip type and the weather; it returns the clothing list.</p></div></div>
        <div class="item"><span class="n">03</span><div><h3 class="item-h">89 Survival Cards</h3><p>Print-at-home cards for the day the packing wasn't the problem — navigation, water, shelter, fire, emergencies, and a seven-day skills challenge.</p></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="price-tag" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <span class="price">$19</span>
        <span class="was">one-time · instant download · both parts</span>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-top:18px;">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={CHECKOUT_URL} rel="noopener">Get TrailReady — $19</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={url('/product/')}>See what's inside</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="product-section">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:74ch;">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>The questions people<br>actually ask</h2>
        <p class="desc">Phrased the way they get asked out loud, and answered in full.</p>
      </div>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>I'm going camping for the first time — what do I actually need to bring?</summary>
        <p>For a first tent or car camping trip you need six things covered: shelter and sleep (tent, pegs, sleeping bag, insulating mat), kitchen and food (stove, fuel, one pan, water container, meals planned by name), clothing in layers with two spare pairs of socks, a head torch for every person, a first aid kit, and someone at home who knows where you are going and when you are back. Everything else is comfort. The full 50-item list on this page is free to read and free to download, with no email required.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>I'm going camping this weekend. What should I pack?</summary>
        <p>Work through a checklist a few days before rather than the night before, because that is when you still have time to fix an empty gas canister or a flat head torch battery. Pack shelter and sleep first, then food planned meal by meal, then clothing in layers including a warm layer for sitting still after dark, then light and safety. Cross off anything that does not apply to this specific trip — a checklist is a memory aid, not an instruction to bring all fifty things.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What do people always forget when camping?</summary>
        <p>The most commonly forgotten items are spare dry socks, enough light for everyone rather than one shared torch, a warm layer for sitting still after sunset, rubbish bags, a spare tent peg and a length of cord, and toilet roll. None of them are expensive and each one is remembered vividly by whoever forgot it.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Is there a free printable camping checklist I can just download?</summary>
        <p>Yes — this page has one. It is a two-page PDF with 50 items across six sections, free with no email address, no signup and no account. It also works as a real form: the checkboxes are genuine PDF form fields, so you can tick them on screen and save the file, or print it and use a pen. Most free printable checklists are flat images whose boxes cannot be ticked at all.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What's the difference between a camping checklist and a backpacking checklist?</summary>
        <p>A camping checklist assumes a car carries the weight, so it can include a cool box, a chair, a bigger tent and a proper cooking set. A backpacking checklist assumes everything goes on your back, which changes every trade-off: you drop comfort items, carry a filter instead of water, and think in grams. Using a car camping list for a backpacking trip is how people end up with a pack too heavy to enjoy.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How do I make a camping checklist for my family?</summary>
        <p>Start from a general list, then add one line per person for the things that are individual — medication, glasses, a comfort toy for a small child, a head torch each. Keep shared items in one place on the list so nobody packs three stoves and no pan. After the trip, edit the list while it is fresh: cross off what you never touched and add what you wished you had. After two or three trips it fits how your family actually camps.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What should I wear camping at night when it gets cold?</summary>
        <p>Pack for sitting still, not for walking. Camping is the one outdoor activity where you spend hours doing almost nothing after dark, which is exactly when the temperature drops fastest — so bring a warmer layer than you think, a hat, and something on your feet other than the boots you walked in. Dry socks matter more than an extra jacket, because feet that got damp during the day will feel cold all evening.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Do I really need a sleeping mat, or is the sleeping bag enough?</summary>
        <p>You need the mat. Cold ground draws heat out of you directly, and your body weight flattens the sleeping bag's insulation underneath you to almost nothing, so the bag cannot do its job where you are lying on it. A modest bag on a good insulating mat is warmer than an expensive bag on a thin one.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How far in advance should I pack for a camping trip?</summary>
        <p>Run the checklist a few days before, then again as you load the car. The early pass is what catches the problems you can still solve — empty stove fuel, dead torch batteries, a tent you have never pitched. The second pass is what stops things being left on the kitchen table.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What is the one thing I should not skip before a camping trip?</summary>
        <p>Tell one person where you are going and when you expect to be back, and send it as a message so it is written down rather than remembered. It costs nothing, weighs nothing, and it is the single thing that most shortens a search if anything goes wrong. Pitching your tent once at home before you go is a close second.</p>
      </details>
      <p style="margin-top:22px;">
        Still stuck? <a href={url('/contact/')}>Ask us</a> — we answer in English and there is no mailing list.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>
